[Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  hey everyone my name is joe sasso i'm a  chef here in los angeles california and  today i'm going to show you my family's  recipe for ultimate lasagna  i wanted to make lasagna because in my  family when you think of the holidays  it's always lasagna whether it's  thanksgiving or christmas sure we'll  have turkey but the main event is always  the lasagna so holiday times  this is the recipe that is closest to my  heart  so mixing together the eggs the flour  and the spinach powder a little bit of  salt kneading that together for about 20  minutes until it's nice and smooth  and then you have to give the gluten to  rest give that some time to relax let  those bonds form really strongly  and that way when you go to roll out  your dough it doesn't bounce back on you  it's nice smooth velvety and supple  there is absolutely nothing wrong with  buying  dried pasta and using that they make  some really good ones out there but  there's something special about making  pasta by hand it's a tactile experience  you're putting  your love and your labor into this  product and then you're sharing it  directly with your family with your  friends  so if you have the opportunity to make  fresh pasta you're doing something  special like lasagna  might as well go the distance and go all  in  the entire sheeting process may look  very intimidating it's not at all if you  have a good dough and it's needed  properly and rested properly the  sheeting process in laminating actually  goes very easily  so as you're cutting your sheets for  your lasagna obviously take a look at  the baking dish that you're going to use  you can use any size that you'd like  just make sure you're cutting your  sheets to fit the size of your baking  dish  bechamel are one of those base sauces  that's used in a number of different  applications all over the world  essentially it's  milk or cream thickened with a roux  flour and butter that acts as our nice  white creamy rich sauce that's going to  go in between the layers of our lasagna  your bechamel should thicken as soon as  it comes to a boil once it's boiling or  simmering you want to give it another  two or three minutes just to cook off  any of that raw flour taste that still  might be in there  so growing up in an italian household  it's all about family and it's all about  food we start eating from the moment we  wake up till the moment we take a nap  then we wake up keep eating again so  lasagna  was always that main event sometimes  we'd have it in the afternoon round two  then we'll have the main dinner with the  turkey or the roast beef and then  maybe another slice of lasagna before  bed but to me lasagna was always that  that center piece  so the biggest thing for me when you're  making gravy is you don't want these  giant meat nuggets all throughout your  sauce  when you meat first goes into the pan  whether it be with a whisk or a spoon  make sure to break up all those chunks  so it's nicely uniformed ground beef and  that way that'll coat your entire pasta  and you won't get these big pockets and  big chunks of meat throughout  to me making and eating lasagna's love  always remember eat pasta make love  and the meat sauce is definitely the  base to the lasagna you know i think  they're you could tell a lot about the  way someone grew up is whether they call  it red sauce or gravy in my family gravy  was this it's tomato sauce with the meat  and the veggies and everything cooked in  it for a number of hours but that's what  gravy is not that brown stuff you put on  roast beef that's different  this is gravy you could call it red  sauce whatever you want to call it it's  really good  so moving into the blanching of the  pasta  have your pot of salted boiling water  those go in for about 30 to 60 seconds  into an ice bath that's going to  immediately stop the cooking process and  transfer it to some parchment paper  sprayed with non-stick spray you want to  make sure to get non-stick spray between  each layer and on top of each layer  otherwise you're going to run into  problems later when you're assembling  assembling the lasagna organization is  going to be the key to happiness while  you're putting together lasagna making  sure all of your ingredients are lined  up room temperature chilled you don't  want to build a lasagna with hot sauce  or hot bechamel it's just going to be a  mess it's not going to work take the  time to do these things ahead and that  way it's all ready to go when it's time  to assemble  don't worry about trimming any  overlapping edges or any pasta hanging  out of the sides  that leads to a golden brown and  delicious rim  your entire family will fight over them  mine did it was the most sought after  part of the entire lasagna  my favorite step is watching it come out  of the oven and then it's a true test of  patience because you can't eat it  immediately you have to let it cool for  i would say at the minimum 15 minutes if  you give it a half an hour that's even  better because it's molten hot in there  and you're doing yourself a favor by  waiting it'll hold together when you go  and cut it and it'll be that much more  enjoyable having to wait  to then eat the pasta  so good  it's like childhood all over again  as an adult  eating the crispy bits it's the part  everybody used to fight over and they're  all mine  oh yes  [Music]  [Music]  you

